From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:00:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ether: VoLTE is a device capability, not a T-Mobile one

config_device_volte_available, config_device_vt_available and config_device_wfc_ims_available were
under values-mcc310-mnc240 and values-mcc310-mnc260, so VoLTE worked on Mint and T-Mobile and was
off everywhere else. Put any other SIM in and the resource fell back to the AOSP default of false.

That is the wrong axis. isVolteEnabledByPlatform() ANDs the device leg with the carrier's
carrier_volte_available_bool: the first says "this hardware can do it", the second says "this
network permits it". Qualifying the device leg by carrier meant every network except the two we had
directories for was refused before its own carrier config was ever consulted -- including carriers
AOSP already ships working VoLTE configuration for.

It was never a decision. The config originally existed only under mnc260, and patch 0025 fixed
VoLTE by adding mnc240 once we found resource qualifiers resolve from the SIM rather than the
serving network. Nobody asked whether it should be qualified at all.

So the device legs move to the unqualified overlay, and the mcc/mnc directories keep only what is
genuinely per-carrier: config_carrier_volte_available and config_volte_replacement_rat, whose value
of 3 is explicitly T-Mobile's.

A carrier whose config says VoLTE is unavailable still gets no VoLTE, via the leg that is supposed
to decide that. Untested on any network but this one, which is rather the point.
---
 .../res/res/values-mcc310-mnc240/config.xml   | 32 ++++++-------------
 .../res/res/values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml   | 30 ++++++-----------
 .../base/core/res/res/values/config.xml       | 18 +++++++++++
 3 files changed, 37 insertions(+), 43 deletions(-)

diff --git a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc240/config.xml b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc240/config.xml
index 60ee34b..b18ed8e 100644
--- a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc240/config.xml
+++ b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc240/config.xml
@@ -1,30 +1,16 @@
 <?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
-<!-- Mint Mobile (MCC 310 / MNC 240) VoLTE / VT / Wi-Fi-Calling.
+<!-- Mint Mobile (MCC 310 / MNC 240). Carrier-specific legs only.
 
-     Mint runs on T-Mobile's network but issues its own SIMs, and Android picks resource
-     mcc/mnc qualifiers from the SIM, not from the serving network. On this device
-     gsm.sim.operator.numeric reads 310240 while gsm.operator.numeric reads 310260, so the
-     values-mcc310-mnc260 overlay never applied and config_device_volte_available fell back to
-     the AOSP default of false. ImsManager.isVolteEnabledByPlatform() ANDs that resource with
-     carrier_volte_available_bool, so VOICE never entered the CapabilityChangeRequest, the
-     vendor stack was only ever told to enable UT over LTE, and the modem never registered.
+     The device-capability booleans (config_device_volte_available and friends) are NOT here: they
+     live unqualified in values/config.xml, because whether this modem can do VoLTE does not depend
+     on the SIM. Only genuinely per-carrier values belong in an mcc/mnc directory.
 
-     Carrier config was not the problem: mConfigFromDefaultApp supplies
-     carrier_volte_available_bool=true for this SIM. Only the device leg was false.
-
-     Keep this file in step with values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml. -->
+     Worth keeping in mind for anything added here: Android resolves these qualifiers from the SIM,
+     not from the serving network. Mint runs on T-Mobile, so gsm.sim.operator.numeric reads 310240
+     while gsm.operator.numeric reads 310260, and a value placed only under mnc260 never applies to
+     a Mint SIM. That cost this port a long time when the device leg lived here. -->
 <resources>
-    <bool name="config_device_volte_available">true</bool>
-    <!-- Video calling is off deliberately, not merely untested. The VT engine lib-imsvt.so
-         imports IOMXObserver and IGraphicBufferAlloc, platform interfaces Android deleted outright
-         when OMX moved to HIDL/Codec2: 61 unresolved symbols, and not shimmable. libshim_vtsurface
-         only gets the JNI layer loading so ImsVideoGlobals.init() can create its singletons; it
-         does not make video work. isVtEnabledByPlatform() ANDs this with the carrier's
-         KEY_CARRIER_VT_AVAILABLE_BOOL, so false here removes the Carrier video calling toggle and
-         stops the framework ever requesting a VT capability. -->
-    <bool name="config_device_vt_available">false</bool>
-    <bool name="config_device_wfc_ims_available">true</bool>
-    <!-- legacy leg some code paths still consult -->
+    <!-- legacy carrier leg some code paths still consult -->
     <bool name="config_carrier_volte_available">true</bool>
     <!-- displayed RAT during a VoLTE call; 3 = UMTS, T-Mobile's value -->
     <integer name="config_volte_replacement_rat">3</integer>
diff --git a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml
index 602887b..a1fc5dc 100644
--- a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml
+++ b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values-mcc310-mnc260/config.xml
@@ -1,26 +1,16 @@
 <?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
-<!-- T-Mobile US (MCC 310 / MNC 260) VoLTE / VT / Wi-Fi-Calling. NOT Mint: Mint SIMs
-     report 310240 and are handled by the values-mcc310-mnc240 sibling, because Android
-     takes these qualifiers from the SIM and not from the serving network.
-     Android applies this values-mcc310-mnc260 overlay ONLY when a 310/260 SIM is present,
-     so it is inherently scoped to T-Mobile's network — no other carrier is touched.
+<!-- T-Mobile US (MCC 310 / MNC 260). Carrier-specific legs only.
 
-     config_device_volte_available is the resource ImsManager.isVolteEnabledByPlatform()
-     actually reads. The old carrier-agnostic patch set config_carrier_volte_available, which
-     that path ignores, so it only ever worked via the persist.dbg global override (dropped
-     here). Paired with the T-Mobile-filtered CarrierConfig bundle in the CarrierConfig overlay. -->
+     The device-capability booleans (config_device_volte_available and friends) are NOT here: they
+     live unqualified in values/config.xml, because whether this modem can do VoLTE does not depend
+     on the SIM. Only genuinely per-carrier values belong in an mcc/mnc directory.
+
+     Worth keeping in mind for anything added here: Android resolves these qualifiers from the SIM,
+     not from the serving network. Mint runs on T-Mobile, so gsm.sim.operator.numeric reads 310240
+     while gsm.operator.numeric reads 310260, and a value placed only under mnc260 never applies to
+     a Mint SIM. That cost this port a long time when the device leg lived here. -->
 <resources>
-    <bool name="config_device_volte_available">true</bool>
-    <!-- Video calling is off deliberately, not merely untested. The VT engine lib-imsvt.so
-         imports IOMXObserver and IGraphicBufferAlloc, platform interfaces Android deleted outright
-         when OMX moved to HIDL/Codec2: 61 unresolved symbols, and not shimmable. libshim_vtsurface
-         only gets the JNI layer loading so ImsVideoGlobals.init() can create its singletons; it
-         does not make video work. isVtEnabledByPlatform() ANDs this with the carrier's
-         KEY_CARRIER_VT_AVAILABLE_BOOL, so false here removes the Carrier video calling toggle and
-         stops the framework ever requesting a VT capability. -->
-    <bool name="config_device_vt_available">false</bool>
-    <bool name="config_device_wfc_ims_available">true</bool>
-    <!-- legacy leg some code paths still consult -->
+    <!-- legacy carrier leg some code paths still consult -->
     <bool name="config_carrier_volte_available">true</bool>
     <!-- displayed RAT during a VoLTE call; 3 = UMTS, T-Mobile's value -->
     <integer name="config_volte_replacement_rat">3</integer>
diff --git a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml
index b58855e..5e72034 100644
--- a/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml
+++ b/overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml
@@ -281,4 +281,22 @@
          colon-separated *string*; a string-array is silently ignored. -->
     <string name="config_defaultListenerAccessPackages" translatable="false">com.nextbit.robinled</string>
 
+    <!-- IMS device capability. This is the "can this hardware do it" leg, and it has nothing to do
+         with which SIM is inserted, so it is deliberately NOT under an mcc/mnc qualifier.
+
+         It used to be, and that was the wrong axis: ImsManager.isVolteEnabledByPlatform() ANDs this
+         with the carrier's carrier_volte_available_bool, so qualifying the device leg by carrier
+         meant every network except the two we had directories for was refused before its own
+         carrier config was ever consulted, including carriers AOSP ships working VoLTE config for.
+         Carrier permission is the carrier leg's job.
+
+         Video calling is off deliberately, not merely untested: lib-imsvt.so imports IOMXObserver
+         and IGraphicBufferAlloc, platform interfaces deleted outright when OMX moved to HIDL and
+         Codec2, among 61 unresolved symbols, and they are not shimmable. libshim_vtsurface exists
+         only so ImsVideoGlobals.init() can construct its singletons. False here removes the carrier
+         video calling toggle and stops the framework requesting a VT capability at all. -->
+    <bool name="config_device_volte_available">true</bool>
+    <bool name="config_device_vt_available">false</bool>
+    <bool name="config_device_wfc_ims_available">true</bool>
+
 </resources>
